::ENG2_U05_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] The root struct means build. Which word means to build again?{
~A. instruct#Incorrect. Instruct means teach or guide.
=B. reconstruct#Correct. Re means again, and construct means build.
~C. structure#Incorrect. Structure is something built or arranged.
~D. constructive#Incorrect. Constructive means helpful or building toward improvement.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word belongs to the construct word family?{
~A. constant#Incorrect. This word does not share the construct meaning.
~B. contrast#Incorrect. This is not part of the construct word family.
~C. consult#Incorrect. This does not share the struct/build meaning.
=D. construction#Correct. Construction is related to building.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] In "constructive feedback," constructive most nearly means:{
=A. helpful#Correct. Constructive means building toward improvement.
~B. harmful#Incorrect. Constructive feedback helps.
~C. unrelated#Incorrect. Constructive feedback relates to improvement.
~D. silent#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Sentence: "The volunteers will reconstruct the outdoor stage." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. The volunteers will ignore the stage.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~B. The volunteers will perform on the stage.#Incorrect. This is not what reconstruct means.
=C. The volunteers will rebuild the outdoor stage.#Correct. Reconstruct means build again.
~D. The volunteers will name the stage.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which pair shows a prefix changing the meaning of a base word?{
=A. construct and reconstruct#Correct. Re changes construct to mean build again.
~B. construct and construction#Incorrect. This changes word form with a suffix, not a prefix.
~C. structure and structural#Incorrect. This uses a suffix change.
~D. teach and teacher#Incorrect. This uses a suffix change.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] What does the prefix re usually mean in reconstruct?{
~A. not#Incorrect. Re does not mean not.
~B. before#Incorrect. Pre usually means before.
~C. across#Incorrect. Trans can mean across.
=D. again#Correct. Re means again in reconstruct.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] What does the suffix -ive suggest in constructive?{
~A. past tense#Incorrect. -ed often marks past tense.
=B. having the quality of#Correct. Constructive has the quality of building or helping.
~C. more than one#Incorrect. Plural endings show number.
~D. opposite of#Incorrect. Prefixes such as un- can show opposite meaning.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which sentence uses instruction correctly?{
~A. The instruction barked loudly.#Incorrect. This does not use the word logically.
~B. The instruction slept after lunch.#Incorrect. Instruction is not a living thing.
=C. Clear instruction helped students understand the assignment steps.#Correct. Instruction means teaching, guidance, or directions.
~D. The instruction was a type of sandwich.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: "Clear instruction helps students understand each step." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Students cannot understand steps.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
~B. Directions confuse every student.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
~C. Steps are not part of instruction.#Incorrect. The original connects instruction and steps.
=D. Clear directions help students know what to do at each stage.#Correct. This keeps the meaning in new words.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which statement about word families is correct?{
=A. Words in a family share a base meaning but can differ by prefix, suffix, and context.#Correct. This explains how word families work.
~B. All words in a family mean exactly the same thing.#Incorrect. Related words have connected but not identical meanings.
~C. Word families are based only on spelling.#Incorrect. Meaning matters.
~D. Context should be ignored when studying word families.#Incorrect. Context helps confirm meaning.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word means something built or arranged?{
~A. instruct#Incorrect. This means teach or guide.
~B. reconstruct#Incorrect. This means build again.
=C. structure#Correct. A structure is something built or arranged.
~D. destructive#Incorrect. This means causing damage.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] In "The essay has a clear structure," structure means:{
~A. a loud sound#Incorrect. This does not fit the sentence.
=B. an organized arrangement#Correct. In this context, structure means organization or arrangement.
~C. a random guess#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. a mistake#Incorrect. This does not fit.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word part in reconstruct means again?{
=A. re#Correct. The prefix re- means again, so it helps explain that a word involves doing something another time.
~B. struct#Incorrect. Struct means build.
~C. ion#Incorrect. -ion often forms a noun.
~D. ive#Incorrect. -ive can mean having the quality of.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Sentence: "The peer review gave constructive comments." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. The peer review gave comments that damaged the writing.#Incorrect. Constructive comments help improve writing.
~B. The peer review gave no comments.#Incorrect. This contradicts the sentence.
~C. The comments were unrelated to the writing.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
=D. The peer review gave helpful comments that could improve the writing.#Correct. This accurately paraphrases constructive in context.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word is a derivation of construct?{
~A. consult#Incorrect. This is not derived from construct.
~B. constant#Incorrect. This is not derived from construct.
=C. constructive#Correct. Constructive is formed from construct with a suffix.
~D. contrast#Incorrect. This is not derived from construct.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which sentence uses reconstruct correctly?{
~A. The class will reconstruct the poem by reading it silently.#Incorrect. This does not use reconstruct clearly.
=B. The class will reconstruct the broken model after it falls.#Correct. This means build again.
~C. The class will reconstruct lunch by eating early.#Incorrect. This is illogical.
~D. The class will reconstruct a question by ignoring it.#Incorrect. This does not match the meaning.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which strategy best confirms a word-part prediction?{
~A. Ignore the sentence and use only the first letter.#Incorrect. This is not reliable.
~B. Assume the longest answer is correct.#Incorrect. Length does not determine meaning.
=C. Check the sentence context to see whether the predicted meaning fits.#Correct. Context confirms whether the word-part meaning works.
~D. Choose a meaning unrelated to the root.#Incorrect. The root is important evidence.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: "The committee will reconstruct the schedule after reviewing feedback." Which paraphrase is best?{
=A. The committee will rebuild or reorganize the schedule after considering feedback.#Correct. This keeps the meaning accurately.
~B. The committee will cancel all feedback.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~C. The committee will ignore the schedule.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
~D. The committee will read the schedule aloud.#Incorrect. This is not the meaning of reconstruct.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word part in construction makes the word a noun?{
~A. con#Incorrect. This is not the noun-forming suffix here.
=B. ion#Correct. -ion often turns a verb idea into a noun.
~C. struct#Incorrect. This is the root.
~D. re#Incorrect. Re is a prefix meaning again.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word best completes the sentence? "The team used feedback to _____ the project plan."{
~A. destruction#Incorrect. This noun does not fit the sentence.
~B. constructive#Incorrect. This adjective does not fit after "to."
~C. instruction#Incorrect. This noun does not fit after "to."
=D. reconstruct#Correct. The verb reconstruct fits after "to" and means rebuild or reorganize.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which meaning is supported by the parts of destructive?{
=A. causing damage or tearing down#Correct. Destructive is connected to the opposite of building or to causing damage.
~B. building again#Incorrect. That meaning fits reconstruct.
~C. giving directions#Incorrect. That meaning fits instruction.
~D. organized arrangement#Incorrect. That meaning fits structure.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase uses word-family knowledge correctly? Sentence: "The editor offered constructive advice."{
~A. The editor offered advice that damaged the draft.#Incorrect. Constructive means helpful, not harmful.
~B. The editor offered no advice.#Incorrect. This contradicts the sentence.
=C. The editor offered helpful advice that could build a stronger draft.#Correct. This uses the build-related meaning of constructive.
~D. The editor offered advice about construction workers.#Incorrect. This misuses the word family.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word is not in the struct/build word family used in this lesson?{
~A. construct#Incorrect. This is in the word family.
~B. reconstruct#Incorrect. This is in the word family.
~C. construction#Incorrect. This is in the word family.
=D. consult#Correct. Consult is not part of the struct/build word family.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which explanation best defines etymology?{
~A. the number of letters in a word#Incorrect. Etymology is not letter count.
=B. the history or origin of a word#Correct. Etymology studies where words come from.
~C. the punctuation around a word#Incorrect. Etymology is about origin.
~D. the sound of a word only#Incorrect. Etymology is broader than sound.
}



::ENG2_U05_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which final strategy is strongest when analyzing an unfamiliar academic word?{
=A. Break the word into parts, connect it to a word family, and use context to confirm meaning.#Correct. This combines etymology, derivation, and context.
~B. Guess from the first letter and stop.#Incorrect. This is not reliable.
~C. Ignore related words and context.#Incorrect. Related words and context are useful evidence.
~D. Assume every word with similar spelling has the same meaning.#Incorrect. Spelling alone is not enough.
}
